# Today in History — Editorial Standard

Today in History is **not** an "On This Day" database. It is a carefully curated
newspaper feature. Kindred never automatically chooses the most famous event.

## Editorial judgment

Evaluate candidates on historical significance, storytelling quality, educational
value, authentic visual quality, curiosity, emotional impact, timelessness,
trusted sources, and overall newspaper appeal. The highest-scoring story **that
can be paired with an authentic visual** becomes Today's History.

Think like a newspaper editor, not a database. Quality over quantity. Curation
over chronology.

## Visual policy — no exceptions

An authentic visual is **required**. Never use AI-generated images, stock
photography, or generic placeholders.

If one event cannot be paired with an authentic visual, choose another event
from that date. Never publish a history card without an image.

Acceptable visuals include historical photographs, public-domain paintings,
illustrations, engravings, statues, monuments, maps, newspaper front pages,
documents, artifacts, spacecraft, and other museum-grade historical media from
Wikimedia Commons, Wikipedia, LOC, NARA, Smithsonian, NPS, and similar archives.

When appropriate, include a subtle photo credit beneath the image.

## Reader experience

Homepage card (entire card tappable):

- Authentic image with credit
- Compelling headline
- One engaging intro paragraph (80–140 words)
- "Continue Reading"

Full article opens in the native reader with rich storytelling, context, why it
still matters, and additional authentic imagery when available.

## Final test

"Would this make a beautiful newspaper page that someone would stop and read
over a cup of coffee?"

